<div class="pattern-strip">
  <div class="pattern-strip-heading" style="color:#34d399;">✅ Proven — documented and sourced</div>
  <div class="pattern-grid">
    <div class="pattern-card pv-green">
      <div class="pattern-card-num">Proven 1 — Woodward's Sheetz Consulting</div>
      <div class="pattern-card-title">The county board chair is a paid consultant for a company with business before local governments</div>
      <div class="pattern-card-body">Dave Woodward works as a paid Sheetz consultant through Woodward &amp; Associates LLC while chairing the Board of Commissioners — appearing for Sheetz at <em>Farmington Hills</em> (voted down; Sheetz sued the city) and <em>Royal Oak</em> (approved) — and refuses to disclose his compensation. <a href="https://www.crainsdetroit.com/politics-policy/dave-woodward-sheetz-oakland-county" target="_blank">Crain's</a> · <a href="https://www.axios.com/local/detroit/2025/08/07/oakland-county-chair-sheetz-consulting-conflict" target="_blank">Axios</a> · Detroit News 7/31/25</div>
    </div>
    <div class="pattern-card pv-green">
      <div class="pattern-card-num">Proven 2 — The County-Land Lever</div>
      <div class="pattern-card-title">"I have been overruled" — the airport easement episode</div>
      <div class="pattern-card-body">WXYZ documented Woodward pushing a Sheetz project adjacent to county-owned airport land in Waterford: airport manager Cheryl Bush's internal email says she was <strong>"overruled"</strong> on an easement she opposed on safety grounds, names Woodward as "a strong proponent," and says he "made introductions to the Sheetz folks." The county declined the easement only after reporters asked. This is the one <em>proven</em> instance of the pattern the golf-course theory imagines — and the template for what to watch on any county-owned land. <a href="https://www.wxyz.com/news/local-news/investigations/did-oakland-county-commission-head-use-position-to-help-along-possible-sheetz-in-waterford" target="_blank">WXYZ investigation</a></div>
    </div>
    <div class="pattern-card pv-green">
      <div class="pattern-card-num">Proven 3 — Springfield's Planner Flip</div>
      <div class="pattern-card-title">CWA hired mid-moratorium, mid-mine-fight — Ben Carlisle pitched it personally</div>
      <div class="pattern-card-body">Two steps: Jan 12, 2026 — CWA takes over Building Department functions. Mar 12, 2026 — the board votes <strong>7-0</strong> to fire Giffels Webster and hire Carlisle/Wortman as planner, with Ben Carlisle presenting (citing CWA experience with <em>"gravel mining and data centers"</em>) and Megan Masson-Minock as primary consultant, championed by Supervisor Ric Davis. This happened with a data center moratorium, documented developer interest, and the Levy mine application all pending — the placement pattern P.A.C.T. tracks statewide, arriving in Oakland County. <a href="https://cms1files.revize.com/springfieldtwp/Minutes_TB_3-12-2026-FINAL.pdf" target="_blank">Mar 12 minutes</a> · <a href="https://cms1files.revize.com/springfieldtwp/Special%20Meeting%201-12-26%20-%20FINAL.pdf" target="_blank">Jan 12 minutes</a></div>
    </div>
    <div class="pattern-card pv-green">
      <div class="pattern-card-num">Proven 4 — Springfield's Moratorium Status</div>
      <div class="pattern-card-title">Extended through Dec 10, 2026 (Resolution 2026-09) — it did not lapse</div>
      <div class="pattern-card-body">The 180-day moratorium adopted in early January 2026 was amended and extended <strong>through December 10, 2026</strong> at the July 9 board meeting, with a revised draft ordinance headed to the Planning Commission. Developer interest is real but pre-application: an ~80-84 acre site off Andersonville Rd, and the township's own Q&amp;A concedes "a general inquiry, not a formal application." The township has no municipal water or sewer. <a href="https://cms1files.revize.com/springfieldtwp/July%202026%20-%20FINAL.pdf" target="_blank">Jul 9 minutes</a> · <a href="https://www.springfield-twp.us/departments/supervisor/resident_questions.php" target="_blank">township Q&amp;A</a></div>
    </div>
    <div class="pattern-card pv-green">
      <div class="pattern-card-num">Proven 5 — Wixom: Sue, Then Watch the Council Retreat</div>
      <div class="pattern-card-title">Federal suit Aug 7 → adopted setback 1,500 ft, not the 2,640 ft legal review had reached → city now says its own new ordinance moots the case</div>
